                                                "sense_id": "44bfc340-e45b-385e-a398-b20b3ead44f5",
                                                "text": "[…]without adding the names of those who were widely known to be responsible for the coinage of such wellerisms."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3cfea682-2901-4fce-bf57-4eaa80f7e97c",
                                                "sense_id": "44bfc340-e45b-385e-a398-b20b3ead44f5",
                                                "text": "Dickens used wellerisms already in his letters prior to writing Pickwick Papers."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "41f43c55-ccc2-499d-94ba-6bdd2210d7a6",
                                                "sense_id": "44bfc340-e45b-385e-a398-b20b3ead44f5",
                                                "text": "2002, Fionnuala Carson Williams, Wellerisms in Ireland, University of Vermont, page 38,\nIn many cases where a wellerism and a folktale are connected the quote in the wellerism is a set sentence like 'Up and at it!' which is well-known apart from the wellerism."
                                            }
